The error:

MongoServerError: bad auth : Authentication failed.

means one of these is wrong:

Username
Password
Connection string
Database user doesn't exist or doesn't have access
Check your connection string

It should look similar to:

mongodb+srv://<username>:<password>@cluster0.rnhnurl.mongodb.net/?retryWrites=true&w=majority&appName=Cluster0

If your username is an email address, the @ must be URL-encoded:

li.huybrecht%40ehb.be

instead of

li.huybrecht@ehb.be
Check your password
Make sure it's the MongoDB Atlas database user's password, not your Atlas account password.
If it contains special characters like @, :, /, ?, #, or %, it must be URL-encoded. An easy way is:
const password = encodeURIComponent(process.env.DB_PASSWORD);

Or simply copy the encoded connection string from Atlas.

Verify the database user

In MongoDB Atlas:

Go to Database Access.
Confirm the user exists.
If you're unsure of the password, click Edit and set a new one.
Update the password in your Render environment variables.
Redeploy your service.
Verify your Render environment variable

Ensure MONGODB_URI contains the updated connection string exactly as intended. A common mistake is changing the password in Atlas but forgetting to update it in Render.

Verify network access

Although a network issue usually produces a different error, it's worth checking:

In Atlas, go to Network Access.
Make sure Render's IPs are allowed, or temporarily allow 0.0.0.0/0 for testing (then restrict access later if appropriate).
